The flat’s recurring conflict over chores stems from three intertwined gaps: (a) no durable, visible schedule that survives memory lapses; (b) unspoken power‑ or stress‑related dynamics that surface when verbal agreements are later re‑interpreted; and (c) one roommate’s recent withdrawal, which makes direct conversation feel risky. To tackle these together, I propose a six‑step protocol. First, convene a brief, neutral meeting where each person can voice any underlying tension or workload stress without accusation. Second, co‑design a visual chore board – either a printed wheel or a shared digital Kanban – that assigns weekly duties and includes a simple swap‑log column. Third, draft a short "communication contract" that outlines how to raise a concern (e.g., a 24‑hour text to the group), a pause‑and‑share pause, and a proportionate consequence for repeated non‑compliance (such as a temporary reassignment). Fourth, introduce a 5‑minute weekly sync (in‑person or via the board’s comment thread) to capture schedule changes and update the board. Fifth, add a quick weekly satisfaction pulse (1‑5 rating) that each roommate records next to the board. Finally, after one month, review the pulse data and the swap log to decide whether tweaks – like a more structured mediation step or a different tool – are needed. This blended approach respects the need for a concrete coordination artifact while also giving space for relational repair and for the avoiding roommate to re‑engage on their own terms.